ked  but then when i deployed things into  production i would get problems like  this the process would start  but then after a little while it would  just fail and then just be restarted  over  and over and over again and it took me a  little while to figure out why  and the reason why this didn't work is  because essentially and i'm dumbing this  down a bit but  my startup routine for these processes  looked something like this  the first thing i did when i started up  was i downloaded the audio files  necessary  for these teleconferencing lines to be  served just like i talked about before  so i tried to make them stateless  then after that was done i would bind  myself to the appropriate port  so the load balancer would know that my  application was now online and would  start servicing more requests  and the problem was this download bit  right here and the reason why is  instead of just downloading the audio  files a particular process needed i was  downloading every audio file ever  created which was  hundreds of gigabytes worth and so  basically in the real world when i would  run this stuff  the process would start and then it  would start downloading hundreds of gigs  of files  and because that takes more than 30  seconds the auto scaling software i had  would say well this application hasn't  bound to the port and it's been 30  seconds  it must not be working so we'll just  kill it and then restart it so it would  never finish  and of course in testing i had all the  files set up locally so i didn't have  this issue  but this is a really hard thing to debug  so again keeping this stuff simple and  keeping it stateless  is really important all right next let's  talk about dev prod parity  so the idea here is you always want to  keep all of your development  environments as similar as possible  so development staging production they  should all be identical now this is one  of those rules that i'm ashamed to admit  i break all the time on purpose  i always 