ars it was a big deal  it was making a lot of people money and  i you know i like money so i was  freaking out a bit  so what i did is i just went on a copy  paste rampage  i don't know if any of you have ever  done this i am certainly guilty of it  so i copy and pasted the [ __ ] out of  this code base and i spent the next i  don't know i want to say  four to five months maybe something like  that playing this constant cat and mouse  game with google  of trying to launch these new really  spammy websites designed to drive  affiliate sales  and in the end it didn't work out but  coming back and thinking about this  experience  this whole process was just brutal and  the reason it was so brutal  is because what i should have done is if  i was gonna do this the right way  i would have variablized and templatized  my one code base because the problem  that i had here wasn't that i just had  to launch all these new websites  the problem was that i wasn't smart  about it i was just in you know it's  constantly stressed state i was going in  there  whenever i added a new feature i would  literally go into all of these code  bases 20 30 code bases  and start copying and pasting code  around as [ __ ] a nightmare obviously  so anyways the lesson learned here is  don't do this it's terrible  all right next up the next factor in the  12-factor application  is all about dependency management now  this is also one of those topics  sounds pretty straightforward right you  have dependencies and you need to  declare and isolate them so how does  this work  well when you're building an application  today the first thing you do is you  declare all your dependencies right so  maybe if you're building a node  application  you have some javascript dependencies  you define somewhere in a package.json  file or something like that  but you list it up the next thing you do  is you isolate those dependencies  so you're gonna take it uh maybe you  install all the dependencies using npm  or w