ithub which triggered the deploy and i  pieced out of there started driving  so about an hour and a half later just  as long as it takes ironically for that  deployment to happen  uh i get this angry phone call from my  partner and he's basically telling me  hey  uh there's no chance you like broke the  like the entire application is there  and i was like no not at all absolutely  not  uh there's no way like i deployed some  code a while ago but that was a long  time ago  it is 100 fine so  later i log in i stop it at mcdonald's  i'm using their free crappy wi-fi  and what i realize of course is i forgot  to update my environment variables that  this was reading from  so instead of altering my production  database i altered my sql lite  database on my laptop which while i mean  that is  useful it is not exactly what i wanted  so we had millions of user requests  hitting this application per second and  it was causing all sorts of issues  because  they was expecting a database column to  exist that didn't  so you know i trashed the whole thing so  anyways  that sort of covers all the 12 factors  now i realize that this has been sort of  a weird talk  but i want to leave you with one thing  to go home with okay the point of all  this  is you know some people are going to say  all right randall you've been a total  idiot  you've committed all these programming  sins and this is your your problem  and here's what i like to think you know  programming isn't black and white right  like  there's a lot of gray area sometimes  it's okay to code things in production i  mean at least for me  you know sometimes it's okay to like  skip writing a test every now and then  you're not gonna die  usually and so i guess the thing that  i'm trying to say is  i make mistakes all the time and i've  been programming for 19 years  and i'm sure all of you make mistakes  and even john carmack one of the best  developers in the world  i'm sure he makes mistakes all the time  too so especially if you're a new  developer  don't worry so much about making  mistakes just worry about learning some  of the patterns to try to avoid them as  much as you can  so anyways with that i just want to say  thank you very much for having me if you  want to see any more crazy stories like  this i have published some of them  on my website you can also check out  octa's website and  tweet octa if you want we have some  really cool articles and stuff about  cyber security and things  like that um and then the final thing i  just want to say is  i had a lot of stores i couldn't tell up  here because they're way too  embarrassing to too many people  so if you want to hear about it come  talk to me later and thank you very much  we'll see you all at the break
 

